-- CISSP Domain 5 (Identity and Access Management) expansion, part B of 2 (sibling file
-- covering 5.1-5.3 is out of scope here). Adds flashcards ci-f-1300..1334, ci-a-430..436,
-- ci-y-270..275 and questions ci-q-1300..1339 for objective 5.4 (authorization mechanisms -
-- DAC/MAC/RBAC/ABAC internals: RuBAC, role engineering, static/dynamic SoD, the ABAC
-- attribute model, XACML architecture, MAC compartments, ACLs vs capability tables,
-- content- vs context-dependent control, RAdAC), objective 5.5 (identity provisioning
-- lifecycle - Joiner-Mover-Leaver, birthright access, recertification campaigns,
-- entitlement creep, the mover process, involuntary-vs-voluntary offboarding, orphaned vs
-- dormant accounts, IGA platforms, provisioning-workflow segregation of duties, service
-- account governance, JIT/just-enough access), and objective 5.6 (authentication systems -
-- Kerberos AS/TGS ticket flow and mutual authentication, the golden ticket attack, OAuth 2.0
-- roles/token types/Authorization Code+PKCE/Client Credentials flows, OIDC ID tokens and
-- discovery, SAML SP- vs IdP-initiated SSO). Deliberately goes beyond, rather than restating,
-- the existing DAC/MAC/RBAC/ABAC basic-definition facts (ci-f-041-deleted/101, ci-q-030/106-
-- 109, ci-a-010..013), identity-lifecycle overview (ci-f-045, ci-q-034), Kerberos clock-skew
-- (ci-f-103, ci-q-112), MFA factor categories (ci-f-104, ci-q-113), termination-delay risk
-- (ci-f-105, ci-q-114), SAML assertion/IdP basics (ci-f-043, ci-q-032), and OAuth-vs-OIDC
-- relationship (ci-f-046, ci-q-033) facts already present under their current ids.

insert into public.flashcards (id, cert, deck, domain, objective, front, back) values
-- ---------- 5.4 authorization mechanisms: core ----------
('ci-f-1300','cissp','core',5,'5.4',$$What does the umbrella term "non-discretionary access control" mean, and which two models fall under it?$$,$$Non-discretionary access control means an individual resource owner cannot decide access on personal whim - the decision is instead driven by organizational policy or the system itself. Both Role-Based Access Control (RBAC, permissions tied to a defined job role) and Rule-Based Access Control (RuBAC, permissions tied to predefined conditional rules) fall under this umbrella, in direct contrast to DAC, where the owner's own discretion is the deciding factor.$$),
('ci-f-1301','cissp','core',5,'5.4',$$What is Rule-Based Access Control (RuBAC), and how does it differ from Role-Based Access Control despite the similar-sounding name?$$,$$RuBAC evaluates a fixed set of IF-THEN conditional rules applied globally to every access request - for example, a firewall ACL rule blocking traffic from a specific address range, or a policy denying interactive logins outside business hours. Unlike RBAC, RuBAC's rules are not built around who the subject is organizationally; the identical rule applies uniformly to any subject matching the rule's conditions, regardless of job role.$$),
('ci-f-1302','cissp','core',5,'5.4',$$What is the difference between top-down and bottom-up role engineering when designing an RBAC role structure?$$,$$Top-down role engineering starts from documented job functions and business processes to define what each role should legitimately need, producing accurate but slow-to-build roles. Bottom-up role engineering (role mining) instead analyzes users' existing permission assignments and algorithmically clusters common patterns into candidate roles - faster to produce, but it risks baking each user's pre-existing over-provisioned access directly into the new role definitions.$$),
('ci-f-1303','cissp','core',5,'5.4',$$What is the difference between Static Separation of Duty (SSD) and Dynamic Separation of Duty (DSD) in RBAC?$$,$$SSD permanently prevents a single user from ever being assigned two conflicting roles at all - for example, a user can never simultaneously hold both "Payment Initiator" and "Payment Approver." DSD is more flexible: it allows a user to hold both conflicting roles, but blocks them from activating both within the same session or transaction, so the conflicting privilege can only ever be exercised at separate times.$$),
('ci-f-1304','cissp','core',5,'5.4',$$What four categories of attributes does an ABAC policy engine typically combine to reach a single access decision?$$,$$Subject attributes (who is asking - department, clearance, job title), resource/object attributes (what is being accessed - classification, owner, data type), environment attributes (surrounding context - time of day, location, network, device posture), and action attributes (what operation is requested - read, write, delete). A policy evaluates all four together dynamically at the moment of the request, rather than relying on a static role assignment made in advance.$$),
('ci-f-1305','cissp','core',5,'5.4',$$In the XACML authorization architecture, what specific role do the PEP, PDP, PIP, and PAP each play?$$,$$The Policy Enforcement Point (PEP) intercepts the access request and enforces whatever decision comes back. The Policy Decision Point (PDP) evaluates the applicable policies against the request and renders a permit or deny decision. The Policy Information Point (PIP) supplies the attribute values (subject, resource, environment data) the PDP needs to evaluate that policy. The Policy Administration Point (PAP) is where administrators author and manage the underlying policies themselves.$$),
('ci-f-1306','cissp','core',5,'5.4',$$Beyond a hierarchical clearance level (Confidential/Secret/Top Secret), what additional mechanism does MAC use to enforce need-to-know?$$,$$Compartments (also called categories) - non-hierarchical labels layered on top of a clearance level, such as "Top Secret/CRYPTO" or "Top Secret/SCI." A subject must hold both the clearance level required AND be formally read into the specific compartment to access a compartmented object; holding a high enough clearance level alone is not sufficient.$$),
('ci-f-1307','cissp','core',5,'5.4',$$Why is DAC considered vulnerable to the "Trojan horse problem"?$$,$$Because access under DAC is granted entirely at the resource owner's discretion, a legitimate, authorized user can be tricked into running malicious code that silently inherits that user's own valid permissions and copies or leaks data the user was authorized to read to an unauthorized party. DAC has no system-enforced label stopping this downstream propagation once the owner has legitimately granted the initial access - the model trusts the subject's own actions completely.$$),
('ci-f-1308','cissp','core',5,'5.4',$$What is the difference between an Access Control List (ACL) and a capability table, given the same underlying access control matrix?$$,$$An ACL is object-centric: it is attached to a resource and lists which subjects may access it and how - effectively one column of the access control matrix. A capability table is subject-centric: it is attached to a subject and lists which objects that subject may access and how - effectively one row of the same matrix. They represent identical access information viewed from opposite directions.$$),
('ci-f-1309','cissp','core',5,'5.4',$$What is the difference between content-dependent and context-dependent access control?$$,$$Content-dependent access control inspects the actual data inside a resource to decide access - for example, a DLP filter blocking an outbound email because it detects credit card numbers in the message body. Context-dependent access control instead relies on surrounding circumstances rather than the data itself - time of day, location, device, or the sequence of prior requests - which is the mechanism ABAC's environment attributes typically implement.$$),
('ci-f-1310','cissp','core',5,'5.4',$$How should administrative overhead and scalability needs typically drive the choice between DAC, RBAC, and ABAC?$$,$$DAC has the lowest initial administrative overhead but scales poorly and inconsistently as an organization grows, since every individual owner manages access independently with no central oversight. RBAC scales well once job functions are stable and well-defined, centralizing administration around a manageable set of roles. ABAC carries the highest upfront complexity - attributes and policies must be defined and maintained - but scales best for large, dynamic, or cloud/zero-trust environments that need fine-grained, context-aware decisions no fixed role can express.$$),
('ci-f-1311','cissp','core',5,'5.4',$$What does Risk-Adaptive Access Control (RAdAC) add on top of a standard ABAC policy?$$,$$RAdAC factors in a real-time, calculated risk score - such as an anomalous login location, degraded device health, or current threat-intelligence conditions - alongside the usual subject, resource, and environment attributes, letting the access decision automatically tighten (e.g., require step-up authentication) or loosen as the assessed risk level changes, rather than relying purely on static, pre-defined attribute rules that never adapt to real-time conditions.$$),
-- 5.4 acronym
('ci-a-430','cissp','acronym',5,'5.4',$$ACL$$,$$Access Control List - an object-centric list attached to a resource specifying which subjects may access it and how; one column of the underlying access control matrix.$$),
('ci-a-431','cissp','acronym',5,'5.4',$$XACML$$,$$eXtensible Access Control Markup Language - an XML-based standard for expressing ABAC policies and the request/response exchanged between a Policy Enforcement Point and a Policy Decision Point.$$),
-- 5.4 feynman
('ci-y-270','cissp','feynman',5,'5.4',$$Explain how an access control matrix, an ACL, and a capability table are really the same information viewed three different ways.$$,$$Model answer: Imagine a giant spreadsheet where every row is a subject (a user) and every column is an object (a file, printer, or database table) - each cell records what that subject can do to that object, like "read" or "read/write." That whole spreadsheet is the access control matrix, and in practice it's usually too large to store as one giant grid, so systems store it in pieces instead. An ACL takes one column of that spreadsheet - one specific object - and stores it attached to that object: "here's everyone who can touch me, and how." A capability table takes one row instead - one specific subject - and stores it attached to that subject, like a keyring: "here's everything I'm allowed to touch, and how." Neither view adds or removes any information; they're just two different filing strategies for the exact same underlying permissions, chosen based on whether it's more convenient to ask "who can access this file?" (ACL) or "what can this user access?" (capability table).$$),
('ci-y-271','cissp','feynman',5,'5.4',$$Explain why a hospital might use RBAC for routine chart access but layer ABAC-style rules on top for viewing a VIP patient's record.$$,$$Model answer: For the vast majority of patient charts, a nurse's or physician's access needs map cleanly onto their job function - "cardiology nurse" needs to see cardiology patients on their floor, and RBAC handles that efficiently with one role assignment covering hundreds of routine cases. But a VIP or high-profile patient's record carries extra risk: gossip, media leaks, or targeted snooping by staff who have no clinical reason to look. A role alone can't express "only if you're currently assigned to this specific patient's care team, and only during your shift, and log every view for review" - that requires evaluating attributes at the moment of the request, like assigned-patient status, shift timing, and department, which is exactly what ABAC-style rules add on top of the broader RBAC baseline. The hospital gets RBAC's low administrative cost for the common case and ABAC's fine-grained, context-aware control layered in only where the extra risk justifies the extra complexity.$$),
-- ---------- 5.5 identity provisioning lifecycle: core ----------
('ci-f-1312','cissp','core',5,'5.5',$$What are the three named stages of the Joiner-Mover-Leaver (JML) identity lifecycle model?$$,$$Joiner covers onboarding a new identity and provisioning its initial access. Mover covers any internal transfer, promotion, or role change, requiring both new access to be granted and prior, no-longer-needed access to be revoked. Leaver covers offboarding, deprovisioning access when the person departs the organization. JML gives identity governance a common vocabulary for the recurring triggers that should drive every access change.$$),
('ci-f-1313','cissp','core',5,'5.5',$$What is "birthright access," and why do organizations provision it through standardized role templates rather than ad hoc grants at onboarding?$$,$$Birthright access is the baseline set of permissions every employee in a given role automatically receives on day one - for example, email, the HR self-service portal, and a shared drive for their department. Using a standardized template instead of an administrator manually deciding grant-by-grant keeps the baseline consistent with least privilege, makes onboarding fast and repeatable, and gives auditors a documented, predictable starting point to compare actual access against.$$),
('ci-f-1314','cissp','core',5,'5.5',$$In a mature access recertification program, who is typically responsible for attesting that a user's existing access is still appropriate, and how often does this usually occur?$$,$$The user's manager, or the data/resource owner, formally reviews and attests (signs off) that each entitlement is still needed - not IT or security, who lack the business context to judge appropriateness. Recertification campaigns typically run on a periodic schedule (often quarterly or annually, sometimes more frequently for highly privileged or regulated access), producing an auditable record of the review.$$),
('ci-f-1315','cissp','core',5,'5.5',$$What is "entitlement creep" (also called access creep), and what typically causes it?$$,$$Entitlement creep is the gradual, unintended accumulation of access rights a user acquires over time - usually because access was granted for a prior role, project, or temporary need and was never revoked afterward. Each individual grant may have been reasonable when made, but without disciplined revocation the user ends up holding far more standing access than their current job requires, violating least privilege.$$),
('ci-f-1316','cissp','core',5,'5.5',$$When an employee transfers to a new department, why must the "mover" process both grant new access AND revoke the access tied to their old role, rather than only granting the new access?$$,$$Granting only the new role's access while leaving the old role's access in place is precisely what produces entitlement creep - the employee ends up with the union of every role they've ever held instead of only what their current job needs. A correctly implemented mover process treats a transfer as simultaneously a mini-onboarding (new access) and a mini-offboarding (old access revoked), keeping the account's permissions matched to the current role at all times.$$),
('ci-f-1317','cissp','core',5,'5.5',$$How does the offboarding process for an involuntary, for-cause termination typically differ from the process for a standard voluntary resignation?$$,$$An involuntary termination is treated as a security-sensitive event requiring same-day, often pre-notification, account disablement, immediate credential revocation, and close coordination between HR, security, and physical security to prevent retaliatory action before the employee is informed. A voluntary resignation, by contrast, usually follows a standard checklist executed over the notice period, with access typically revoked at the end of the employee's final scheduled work day rather than immediately.$$),
('ci-f-1318','cissp','core',5,'5.5',$$What is the difference between an orphaned account and a dormant (stale) account?$$,$$An orphaned account is one whose associated person has left the organization or changed roles, yet the account remains active with no valid owner - a pure process failure in deprovisioning. A dormant (stale) account, by contrast, still belongs to a currently valid, active identity, but has simply gone unused for an extended period; it is not necessarily a process failure, but it still represents unnecessary attack surface and should be reviewed or disabled if it is not needed.$$),
('ci-f-1319','cissp','core',5,'5.5',$$What role does an Identity Governance and Administration (IGA) platform play across the identity lifecycle?$$,$$An IGA platform centralizes and automates the workflows spanning the entire JML lifecycle - triggering provisioning from an authoritative HR feed, routing access requests through approval, running scheduled recertification campaigns, and enforcing deprovisioning on departure - replacing what would otherwise be a patchwork of manual tickets and spreadsheets with a single auditable system of record for who has access to what and why.$$),
('ci-f-1320','cissp','core',5,'5.5',$$Why should the person requesting new access for themselves never also be the person who approves that request?$$,$$This is a segregation-of-duties control applied to the provisioning workflow itself: if a requestor could also approve their own request, they could grant themselves any access they wanted with no independent check. Routing every access request to a manager, resource owner, or separate approver ensures someone with the appropriate authority and business context validates the need before access is granted.$$),
('ci-f-1321','cissp','core',5,'5.5',$$How does the lifecycle of a service (non-human) account typically differ from a human identity's lifecycle?$$,$$A service account has no "mover" stage in the human sense and often has no single obvious individual owner unless governance explicitly assigns one, making it easy for such accounts to be forgotten and never reviewed. Because service accounts frequently hold broad, standing privileges to integrate systems, mature programs require an assigned human owner, periodic ownership and necessity review, and explicit retirement when the integration it supports is decommissioned - otherwise service accounts become long-lived, unmonitored, high-privilege targets.$$),
('ci-f-1322','cissp','core',5,'5.5',$$What is just-in-time (JIT) or just-enough-access provisioning, and what problem does it solve compared to granting standing access up front?$$,$$JIT provisioning grants a specific privilege only for the duration it is actually needed - for example, temporarily elevating a user to administrator for a two-hour maintenance window, then automatically revoking it - rather than leaving that privilege permanently active on the account. This sharply reduces the standing attack surface available to an attacker who compromises the account at an arbitrary moment, since most of the time the elevated privilege simply does not exist to be abused.$$),
('ci-f-1323','cissp','core',5,'5.5',$$What is the difference between a user access review and a role (entitlement) review?$$,$$A user access review examines one specific person's accumulated access and asks "does this individual still need everything they currently hold?" A role/entitlement review instead examines one specific role or permission itself and asks "does everyone who holds this role, or this specific entitlement, still legitimately need it, and is the role definition itself still accurate?" Both are needed - a clean role definition can still be attached to the wrong people, and a well-assigned population can still be following an outdated role definition.$$),
-- 5.5 acronym
('ci-a-432','cissp','acronym',5,'5.5',$$JML$$,$$Joiner-Mover-Leaver - the identity lifecycle model naming the three organizational events (onboarding, internal role change, and departure) that should trigger a corresponding access change.$$),
('ci-a-433','cissp','acronym',5,'5.5',$$IGA$$,$$Identity Governance and Administration - a platform category that centralizes and automates provisioning, access requests/approvals, periodic recertification, and deprovisioning across an organization's identity lifecycle.$$),
-- 5.5 feynman
('ci-y-272','cissp','feynman',5,'5.5',$$Explain the Joiner-Mover-Leaver model to someone unfamiliar with IAM, using a single employee's whole career at a company as the example.$$,$$Model answer: Picture one employee's full journey. On day one - the "joiner" moment - HR creates their record, and that automatically provisions a standardized starter kit of access matched to their role: email, the shared drive for their team, the tools their job template says they need. Two years later they get promoted into a different department - the "mover" moment - and a correctly run process does two things at once: grants the access their new role needs, and just as importantly, revokes the access tied to their old role that they no longer need. If that second half gets skipped, they quietly accumulate permissions from every job they've ever held, which is exactly the entitlement creep problem recertification campaigns exist to catch later. Finally, years after that, they resign - the "leaver" moment - and their account gets disabled and eventually deleted on a standard timeline (or immediately, if the departure were for cause instead). JML is just a name for these three predictable trigger points, so that every organizational event that should change someone's access actually does.$$),
('ci-y-273','cissp','feynman',5,'5.5',$$Explain why "the mover problem" (accumulated access from role changes) is often a bigger real-world risk than a slow offboarding process.$$,$$Model answer: A slow leaver process is a known, bounded risk - eventually someone notices the employee is gone (payroll stops, a manager asks, an audit runs), and the account gets disabled; the exposure window, while dangerous, has a natural endpoint once someone pays attention. The mover problem is sneakier because nothing about it looks broken: the employee is still actively working, still logging in every day, still doing legitimate things with their account, so there's no obvious anomaly to trigger anyone's attention. Meanwhile every transfer, promotion, or project assignment that only ever adds access and never revokes the old grant quietly compounds, and a long-tenured employee who has moved through several roles can end up holding a sprawling, undocumented combination of privileges that no single person consciously approved and no one is actively monitoring - a much larger and more silent blast radius if that one account is ever compromised, precisely because it never looked like a problem in the first place.$$),
-- ---------- 5.6 implement and manage authentication systems: core ----------
('ci-f-1324','cissp','core',5,'5.6',$$What is the difference between the Kerberos Authentication Service (AS) and the Ticket Granting Service (TGS), both part of the Key Distribution Center (KDC)?$$,$$The AS handles a client's very first authentication: it verifies the initial credentials and, if valid, issues a Ticket Granting Ticket (TGT). The TGS then handles every subsequent request during that session: the client presents its TGT to the TGS to obtain individual service tickets for specific application servers, without ever re-entering a password. Splitting the two lets the user's password be used only once, at the very start.$$),
('ci-f-1325','cissp','core',5,'5.6',$$Walk through the two-step ticket exchange a Kerberos client performs before it can access an application server.$$,$$First, the client authenticates to the AS and receives a Ticket Granting Ticket (TGT), proving it already authenticated successfully. Second, the client presents that TGT to the TGS and requests a service ticket specific to the application server it wants to reach; the TGS validates the TGT and issues that service ticket. Only then does the client present the service ticket directly to the target application server to establish an authenticated session.$$),
('ci-f-1326','cissp','core',5,'5.6',$$What does it mean that Kerberos provides mutual authentication, and why does that matter?$$,$$Mutual authentication means both the client proves its identity to the server AND the server proves its identity back to the client, using tickets encrypted with keys only the legitimate KDC and target service know. This protects against a rogue or spoofed server impersonating a legitimate service to harvest credentials or data - a risk that protocols providing only one-directional (client-to-server) authentication do not address.$$),
('ci-f-1327','cissp','core',5,'5.6',$$Why is the Key Distribution Center (KDC) considered a single point of failure in Kerberos, and what is a "golden ticket" attack?$$,$$Every authentication in the realm depends on the KDC being available and its master key (the KRBTGT account's key) remaining secret - if the KDC is down, no one can obtain new tickets, and every existing ticket eventually expires. A golden ticket attack occurs when an attacker steals the KRBTGT key itself, letting them forge a fully valid, arbitrarily privileged TGT for any account without ever needing that account's actual password, effectively minting unlimited trusted access.$$),
('ci-f-1328','cissp','core',5,'5.6',$$What are the four roles OAuth 2.0 defines, and how do they relate to each other?$$,$$The resource owner is the user who owns the protected data. The client is the application requesting access on the resource owner's behalf. The authorization server authenticates the resource owner and issues access tokens. The resource server hosts the protected data and accepts access tokens to grant access. The client never sees the resource owner's actual credentials - it only ever receives a scoped access token from the authorization server.$$),
('ci-f-1329','cissp','core',5,'5.6',$$What is the difference between an OAuth 2.0 access token and a refresh token?$$,$$An access token is a short-lived credential a client presents to a resource server to access protected data, deliberately kept short-lived to limit damage if it leaks. A refresh token is longer-lived and is presented back to the authorization server, not the resource server, to obtain a new access token without forcing the resource owner to log in again - letting sessions stay usable over time without keeping a long-lived, high-value credential in active circulation.$$),
('ci-f-1330','cissp','core',5,'5.6',$$What problem does PKCE (Proof Key for Code Exchange) solve for OAuth 2.0's Authorization Code flow, and why do mobile apps and single-page web apps need it?$$,$$Public clients like mobile apps and single-page web apps cannot securely store a client secret - anything embedded in the app or shipped to the browser can be extracted. PKCE has the client generate a random secret ("code verifier") and send only its hash ("code challenge") when starting the flow; the authorization server later requires the original verifier before exchanging the authorization code for a token, preventing an attacker who intercepts the authorization code alone from completing the exchange.$$),
('ci-f-1331','cissp','core',5,'5.6',$$When is OAuth 2.0's Client Credentials flow used, and how does it differ from the Authorization Code flow?$$,$$The Client Credentials flow is used for machine-to-machine (service-to-service) authorization where there is no human resource owner involved at all - the client authenticates directly to the authorization server using its own credentials and receives an access token scoped to itself. This differs from the Authorization Code flow, which exists specifically to let a client obtain delegated access to a human resource owner's data with that owner's explicit involvement and consent.$$),
('ci-f-1332','cissp','core',5,'5.6',$$What is an OpenID Connect (OIDC) ID token, and how does it differ from an OAuth 2.0 access token?$$,$$An ID token is a JWT issued by the authorization server (acting as an OIDC provider) that asserts who authenticated and details of that authentication event (who, when, how) - it is meant to be consumed and validated by the client application itself, proving the user's identity. An OAuth 2.0 access token, by contrast, is meant for the resource server and authorizes API access to protected data; it does not, by itself, reliably identify who the user is.$$),
('ci-f-1333','cissp','core',5,'5.6',$$What do the OIDC discovery document and the UserInfo endpoint each provide?$$,$$The discovery document (published at a well-known URL) is a machine-readable JSON file listing an OIDC provider's configuration - its authorization and token endpoint URLs, supported scopes, and signing keys - letting a client auto-configure itself instead of hardcoding provider details. The UserInfo endpoint is a separate API a client calls, presenting its access token, to retrieve additional profile claims about the authenticated user beyond what the compact ID token already contains.$$),
('ci-f-1334','cissp','core',5,'5.6',$$What is the difference between SP-initiated and IdP-initiated SAML SSO?$$,$$In SP-initiated SSO, the user starts at the Service Provider (the application they want to use), which redirects them to the Identity Provider to authenticate before returning them to the application. In IdP-initiated SSO, the user starts at the Identity Provider's own portal (such as a corporate SSO dashboard) and clicks a link that sends them, already authenticated, directly to the Service Provider - the flow's starting point is the key difference, not which party ultimately issues the assertion.$$),
-- 5.6 acronym
('ci-a-434','cissp','acronym',5,'5.6',$$KDC$$,$$Key Distribution Center - the trusted Kerberos server that houses the Authentication Service (AS) and Ticket Granting Service (TGS), issuing Ticket Granting Tickets and service tickets to authenticated clients.$$),
('ci-a-435','cissp','acronym',5,'5.6',$$JWT$$,$$JSON Web Token - a compact, digitally signed token format commonly used to carry OpenID Connect ID tokens and many OAuth 2.0 access tokens, letting a recipient verify the token's claims and authenticity without a separate lookup.$$),
('ci-a-436','cissp','acronym',5,'5.6',$$PKCE$$,$$Proof Key for Code Exchange - an OAuth 2.0 extension that protects the Authorization Code flow for public clients (mobile apps, single-page apps) that cannot securely hold a client secret, by binding the code exchange to a locally generated secret only the original client possesses.$$),
-- 5.6 feynman
('ci-y-274','cissp','feynman',5,'5.6',$$Explain when an enterprise would choose SAML over OAuth 2.0/OIDC for single sign-on, and vice versa, using concrete application types.$$,$$Model answer: SAML grew up in the era of browser-based enterprise web applications - think logging into a corporate HR portal or a legacy line-of-business web app through a company SSO page - and it's still a strong fit whenever the applications involved are traditional server-rendered web apps that can handle exchanging XML assertions through browser redirects, especially in large enterprises with existing SAML-speaking identity providers. OAuth 2.0 and OIDC, by contrast, were built for a world of REST APIs, mobile apps, and single-page JavaScript applications, where lightweight JSON tokens and mechanisms like PKCE fit naturally into how those clients already talk to backend services - trying to bolt XML-based SAML onto a mobile app is awkward and heavyweight by comparison. In practice, a lot of modern enterprises run both: SAML for legacy or vendor SaaS web portals that only speak SAML, and OIDC for anything mobile, API-driven, or newly built, sometimes even bridging the two through an identity provider that federates both protocols to the same underlying directory.$$),
('ci-y-275','cissp','feynman',5,'5.6',$$Explain why OAuth 2.0 alone cannot reliably tell an application who the user is, and what OpenID Connect adds to fix that.$$,$$Model answer: OAuth 2.0 was designed to solve delegated authorization - letting an app get permission to act on a user's behalf against some API, like reading a calendar - and the access token it hands back is really just a permission slip scoped to that API. The access token's format and contents are intentionally left up to whatever authorization server issued it, so a client application receiving one has no standardized, reliable way to extract "who is this" from it, and worse, some developers historically misused access tokens as a stand-in proof of login, which is a fragile and insecure pattern since access tokens aren't designed to be safely parsed by the client itself. OpenID Connect fixes this by adding a second, purpose-built token issued alongside the access token: the ID token, a standardized JWT whose entire job is to assert "this specific user authenticated, at this time, using this method" in a format the client application is explicitly meant to read and verify. In short, the access token answers "what can this app do," and OIDC's ID token answers "who is this," which is exactly the piece OAuth alone was never designed to provide.$$);

insert into public.questions (id, cert, domain, objective, qtype, difficulty, stem, choices, answer, explanation) values
-- ---------- 5.4 authorization mechanisms ----------
('ci-q-1300','cissp',5,'5.4','mcq',1,$q$A corporate firewall applies the identical rule "deny all inbound connections from IP addresses outside the corporate VPN range" to every user and system attempting to connect, regardless of who or what they are. Which access control model does this rule-driven enforcement represent?$q$,$q$["Rule-Based Access Control (RuBAC)","Role-Based Access Control (RBAC)","Discretionary Access Control (DAC)","Mandatory Access Control (MAC)"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$RuBAC applies a predefined set of conditional rules uniformly to every request, independent of the subject's organizational role or an owner's personal discretion - exactly what this IP-range firewall rule does. RBAC would instead key off job role, DAC off an individual owner's decision, and MAC off classification labels and clearances, none of which describe a rule applied identically to everyone.$q$),
('ci-q-1301','cissp',5,'5.4','mcq',2,$q$An organization's RBAC configuration permanently prevents any single employee from ever being assigned both the "Payables Clerk" role and the "Payables Approver" role at the same time, even temporarily. Which access control concept does this configuration enforce?$q$,$q$["Static Separation of Duty (SSD)","Dynamic Separation of Duty (DSD)","Rule-Based Access Control (RuBAC)","Attribute-Based Access Control (ABAC)"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$SSD blocks a conflicting role combination from ever being assigned to the same user in the first place, which matches the permanent prevention described. DSD would instead permit holding both roles but block activating both within the same session or transaction; RuBAC and ABAC describe different access-decision mechanisms entirely, not role-conflict prevention.$q$),
('ci-q-1302','cissp',5,'5.4','mcq',2,$q$A cloud storage policy grants read access to a financial report only when the requester belongs to the finance department, the report is classified "internal," the request originates from a corporate-managed laptop, and the action requested is "view" rather than "download." Which single category of attributes evaluated together does this scenario best illustrate?$q$,$q$["Subject, resource, environment, and action attributes evaluated together under ABAC","A single subject attribute evaluated alone under RBAC","A single resource attribute evaluated alone under MAC","A rule applied without reference to any attributes under RuBAC"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$This scenario combines who is asking (finance department - subject), what is being accessed (an "internal" report - resource), the surrounding context (corporate-managed laptop - environment), and the specific operation requested (view vs. download - action) - the four-attribute combination that defines an ABAC decision, rather than any single attribute type evaluated on its own.$q$),
('ci-q-1303','cissp',5,'5.4','matching',2,$q$Match each XACML architecture component on the left to its defining function on the right.$q$,$q${"left":["Policy Enforcement Point (PEP)","Policy Decision Point (PDP)","Policy Information Point (PIP)","Policy Administration Point (PAP)"],"right":["Supplies the attribute values the decision engine needs to evaluate a request","Authors and manages the access control policies themselves","Intercepts the access request and enforces whatever decision is returned","Evaluates the applicable policies against the request and renders a permit or deny decision"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[2,3,0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$The PEP sits in the request path, intercepting the request and enforcing the eventual decision; the PDP is the evaluation engine, applying policy to render permit/deny; the PIP is a data source, feeding the PDP the attribute values it needs; and the PAP is purely administrative, where policies are authored and managed rather than evaluated in real time.$q$),
('ci-q-1304','cissp',5,'5.4','mcq',3,$q$An analyst holds a Top Secret clearance but has not been formally read into the "Top Secret/SCI" compartment covering a specific intelligence program. Under MAC, can the analyst access a document labeled Top Secret/SCI for that program?$q$,$q$["No - the clearance level alone is insufficient; the analyst must also be read into the specific compartment","Yes - Top Secret clearance automatically grants access to all compartments at or below that level","Yes, but only if the analyst's manager grants discretionary approval","No, because MAC does not support compartments, only hierarchical clearance levels"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$MAC's need-to-know enforcement layers non-hierarchical compartments (categories) on top of the hierarchical clearance ladder; a subject must satisfy both the required clearance level AND formal read-in to the specific compartment. Clearance alone never substitutes for compartment access, discretionary manager approval has no role in MAC's system-enforced labeling, and compartments are a real, standard part of the MAC model.$q$),
('ci-q-1305','cissp',5,'5.4','mcq',2,$q$An employee with legitimate read access to a sensitive spreadsheet unknowingly runs a malicious macro embedded in an unrelated document. The macro silently copies the spreadsheet's contents and emails them to an external address, using the employee's own valid permissions to do so. What DAC weakness does this scenario illustrate?$q$,$q$["The Trojan horse problem - malicious code inherits the legitimate user's own discretionary permissions to leak data the owner never intended to release","A failure of Mandatory Access Control labeling, since DAC always relies on classification levels","A Rule-Based Access Control misconfiguration in the firewall","An ABAC policy that failed to evaluate the environment attribute correctly"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Because DAC access is granted at the resource owner's discretion with no system-enforced label following the data afterward, code running under a legitimate user's identity inherits that user's own valid permissions and can act on them without any additional check - the defining Trojan horse weakness of DAC. MAC, RuBAC, and ABAC are simply different, unrelated models, not the mechanism at fault here.$q$),
('ci-q-1306','cissp',5,'5.4','mcq',1,$q$A file server stores, attached to each individual file, a list of exactly which users may read or write that specific file. What is this per-object access structure called?$q$,$q$["An Access Control List (ACL)","A capability table","A Security Target","A Protection Profile"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$An ACL is attached to the object (the file) and lists the subjects permitted to access it and how - the object-centric view of the access control matrix. A capability table would instead be attached to a subject, listing every object that subject can access; Security Targets and Protection Profiles are unrelated Common Criteria evaluation terms.$q$),
('ci-q-1307','cissp',5,'5.4','mcq',3,$q$An identity team builds its new RBAC role structure entirely by algorithmically analyzing each employee's current, already-assigned permissions and clustering the common patterns into roles, without first reviewing what each job function should actually require. What risk does this bottom-up approach primarily introduce?$q$,$q$["Existing over-provisioned or unnecessary access held by employees today gets baked directly into the new role definitions","The resulting roles will be too restrictive, blocking employees from work they are supposed to do","The organization will be unable to assign any employee to more than one role","MAC-style compartments will be applied incorrectly to the new roles"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Bottom-up role mining derives roles from what people currently have, not from what they should have - any accumulated excess access already sitting on user accounts gets faithfully reproduced as "normal" in the new role rather than corrected, which is why top-down, requirements-driven role engineering (or at least a cleanup pass before mining) is generally preferred. The other options describe unrelated or fabricated consequences.$q$),
('ci-q-1308','cissp',5,'5.4','mcq',1,$q$A data loss prevention tool scans the body of every outbound email and automatically blocks any message that contains what appears to be a valid credit card number, regardless of who the sender is or when the message is sent. What type of access control decision does this represent?$q$,$q$["Content-dependent access control - the decision is based on inspecting the resource's actual data","Context-dependent access control - the decision is based on surrounding circumstances rather than the data itself","Role-Based Access Control - the decision is based on the sender's assigned job role","Mandatory Access Control - the decision is based on a classification label attached to the email"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Content-dependent access control makes its decision by examining the data itself, exactly as this DLP scan does by inspecting the message body for a credit card pattern - independent of sender identity, role, or time. Context-dependent control would instead key off surrounding circumstances like time or location rather than the content, and neither RBAC nor MAC is described here since no role or classification label is being evaluated.$q$),
('ci-q-1309','cissp',5,'5.4','mcq',3,$q$An access control system normally grants a contractor read-only access to a shared drive, but automatically demands step-up multi-factor authentication and temporarily restricts access to a smaller subset of files after detecting that the contractor's login originated from a new country and an unmanaged device flagged by threat intelligence as recently compromised. What does this dynamic, risk-driven tightening of access illustrate?$q$,$q$["Risk-Adaptive Access Control (RAdAC), which factors a real-time calculated risk score into the access decision alongside standard attributes","Static Separation of Duty, which permanently blocks conflicting role assignments","Rule-Based Access Control, which applies the same fixed rule regardless of context","Mandatory Access Control, which relies solely on a pre-assigned classification label"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$RAdAC layers a real-time, dynamically calculated risk score - drawing on signals like unusual geography, device posture, and threat intelligence - on top of the usual subject/resource/environment attributes, letting the decision tighten or loosen as conditions change, exactly as described. SSD concerns role-conflict prevention, RuBAC applies static rules without weighing a calculated risk score, and MAC relies on fixed labels rather than real-time risk signals.$q$),
('ci-q-1310','cissp',5,'5.4','multi',2,$q$Select the TWO statements below that correctly describe administrative overhead and scalability tradeoffs among DAC, RBAC, and ABAC.$q$,$q$["DAC has the lowest initial administrative overhead but becomes inconsistent and hard to audit as an organization scales","ABAC requires significant upfront investment in defining attributes and policies but scales well for large, dynamic, context-aware environments","MAC is the easiest model to administer in a fast-growing startup with frequently changing access needs","RBAC eliminates the need for any periodic access review once roles are initially defined"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$DAC's low upfront cost comes at the price of consistency and auditability as an organization grows, since each owner manages access independently, while ABAC's heavier initial policy-authoring investment pays off in fine-grained scalability for dynamic environments. MAC is a poor fit for a fast-changing startup, since its rigid, centrally administered clearance/label structure suits high-assurance, stable environments far better than rapid change, and RBAC roles still require periodic recertification - defining roles once does not eliminate the need to verify people still belong in them, which is exactly how entitlement creep accumulates.$q$),
('ci-q-1311','cissp',5,'5.4','ordering',2,$q$Order the steps of a typical XACML access request evaluation, from the moment a subject makes a request to the moment the decision is enforced.$q$,$q$["The Policy Enforcement Point (PEP) intercepts the access request from the subject","The PEP forwards the request to the Policy Decision Point (PDP) for a decision","The PDP queries the Policy Information Point (PIP) for the attribute values it needs","The PDP evaluates the applicable policy and returns a permit or deny decision to the PEP","The PEP enforces the returned decision, allowing or blocking the request"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3,4]$q$::jsonb,$q$XACML evaluation begins where the request enters the system, at the PEP, which delegates the actual decision-making to the PDP; the PDP in turn pulls in whatever attribute data it needs from the PIP before it can evaluate policy, renders its permit/deny decision back to the PEP, and only then does the PEP itself carry out the enforcement action - the PDP decides, but only the PEP actually acts.$q$),
('ci-q-1312','cissp',5,'5.4','mcq',2,$q$An auditor notes that both Role-Based Access Control and Rule-Based Access Control are classified as "non-discretionary," while Discretionary Access Control is not. What specifically makes RBAC and RuBAC non-discretionary?$q$,$q$["Access decisions are driven by organizational policy or predefined rules rather than by an individual resource owner's personal judgment","RBAC and RuBAC never require any administrator to configure access at all","RBAC and RuBAC are only usable in classified government environments","RBAC and RuBAC both rely exclusively on hierarchical clearance levels, like MAC"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$"Non-discretionary" means the individual resource owner does not personally decide who gets access on a case-by-case basis - instead, role assignments or predefined rules, set by policy, drive the decision, unlike DAC where the owner's own discretion is the deciding factor. Both models still require administrative configuration, neither is restricted to government use, and neither relies on MAC's hierarchical clearance labels.$q$),
-- ---------- 5.5 identity provisioning lifecycle ----------
('ci-q-1313','cissp',5,'5.5','mcq',1,$q$A new hire's account is automatically granted a predefined bundle of access - email, the department's shared drive, and the standard productivity suite - the moment their role is set to "Marketing Associate" in the identity system, without any administrator manually selecting individual permissions. What is this predefined access bundle called?$q$,$q$["Birthright access","Just-in-time access","Dynamic Separation of Duty","A capability table"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Birthright access is the standardized baseline bundle a role template automatically grants at onboarding, keeping provisioning fast, consistent, and aligned with least privilege rather than left to case-by-case administrator judgment. Just-in-time access instead grants a privilege temporarily for a specific task; DSD is an RBAC role-conflict control; a capability table is an access-control-matrix representation, not a provisioning concept.$q$),
('ci-q-1314','cissp',5,'5.5','mcq',2,$q$During a quarterly access recertification campaign, who is generally in the best position to determine whether a specific employee still legitimately needs a given entitlement, and is therefore assigned to attest to it?$q$,$q$["The employee's manager or the resource/data owner, who has the business context to judge continued need","The IT help desk, since they originally provisioned the access","The employee themselves, since they know their own job duties best","Whichever auditor is currently reviewing the organization's compliance posture"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Recertification requires business judgment about continued need, not just technical knowledge of what was provisioned - that judgment belongs to the manager or resource owner who understands the employee's current responsibilities. IT typically lacks that business context, self-attestation removes the independent check the whole process exists to provide, and an auditor's role is to verify the recertification process occurred, not to personally attest to each entitlement.$q$),
('ci-q-1315','cissp',5,'5.5','mcq',1,$q$An employee who has worked in three different departments over five years still retains full access to systems from all three roles, even though their current job only requires access to the third. No single grant was made improperly at the time it was issued. What term describes this accumulated, unrevoked access?$q$,$q$["Entitlement creep (access creep)","Birthright access","A Trojan horse attack","A dynamic separation of duty violation"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Entitlement creep describes exactly this pattern - individually reasonable grants that accumulate over time because prior access was never revoked when it was no longer needed, leaving the user with far more standing access than their current role requires. Birthright access describes only the initial onboarding bundle, a Trojan horse is a DAC-specific malicious-code concept, and this scenario involves no conflicting role assignment, so DSD does not apply.$q$),
('ci-q-1316','cissp',5,'5.5','mcq',3,$q$An employee transfers from the Sales department to the Finance department. IT grants the employee all of Finance's standard access but leaves their prior Sales-system access untouched "in case it's still needed." What is the primary risk with this approach?$q$,$q$["The employee now holds the union of both roles' access rather than only what their current job requires, directly contributing to entitlement creep","The employee will be unable to perform any Finance duties until Sales access is manually removed first","This approach satisfies Static Separation of Duty by design","No risk exists, since Sales and Finance access do not typically conflict with each other"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A correctly run mover process revokes the prior role's access at the same time it grants the new role's access, treating a transfer as both a mini-onboarding and a mini-offboarding; leaving old access in place "just in case" is precisely how entitlement creep accumulates over a career. Retaining unrelated Sales access does not itself satisfy SSD (which concerns preventing conflicting role combinations, not general cleanup) and the risk exists regardless of whether the two specific systems technically conflict.$q$),
('ci-q-1317','cissp',5,'5.5','mcq',2,$q$An employee is terminated for cause following a workplace investigation. Compared to a colleague who resigned voluntarily with two weeks' notice, how should the terminated employee's account deprovisioning timeline typically differ?$q$,$q$["Same-day, often pre-notification, disablement coordinated closely with HR and physical security, rather than the standard end-of-notice-period timeline used for a voluntary resignation","Deprovisioning should actually take longer, to allow time for a full audit of everything the employee ever accessed","There should be no difference at all between the two scenarios' timelines","The account should be left active for 30 days after either type of departure to preserve email continuity"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$An involuntary, for-cause termination carries elevated insider-threat risk, so mature offboarding processes disable access immediately, often just before or simultaneous with notifying the employee, coordinated across HR and physical security. A standard voluntary resignation with a notice period typically follows the ordinary checklist, revoking access at the end of the final scheduled work day - treating both cases identically ignores the materially different risk profile.$q$),
('ci-q-1318','cissp',5,'5.5','mcq',1,$q$An account remains enabled and is still tied to a current, actively employed staff member's HR record, but no login has occurred on that account in fourteen months. What best describes this account, as distinct from an orphaned account?$q$,$q$["A dormant (stale) account - it belongs to a currently valid identity but has simply gone unused","An orphaned account - its associated person has left the organization","A birthright account - it was granted automatically at onboarding","A service account - it is used for system-to-system integration rather than a person"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A dormant or stale account is still tied to a legitimate, currently active identity - the defining trait distinguishing it from an orphaned account, whose associated person has actually left or changed roles while the account was never disabled. Nothing in the scenario indicates the account was auto-provisioned at onboarding (birthright describes an access bundle, not an unused-account condition) or that it serves a system integration rather than a person.$q$),
('ci-q-1319','cissp',5,'5.5','mcq',2,$q$An organization replaces its patchwork of manual access-request emails, spreadsheet-tracked recertification, and ad hoc offboarding tickets with a single platform that automatically triggers provisioning from the HR system, routes every access request through approval, schedules recertification campaigns, and enforces deprovisioning on departure. What category of platform is this?$q$,$q$["Identity Governance and Administration (IGA)","A Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) platform","A Certificate Authority (CA)","A Configuration Management Database (CMDB)"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$IGA platforms exist specifically to centralize and automate the workflows spanning the full identity lifecycle - provisioning, access requests, certification, and deprovisioning - replacing manual, inconsistent processes with a single auditable system. A SIEM aggregates security event logs, a CA issues digital certificates, and a CMDB tracks asset/configuration records - none manage the identity lifecycle workflow itself.$q$),
('ci-q-1320','cissp',5,'5.5','mcq',3,$q$A manager submits a request granting themselves administrative access to a financial system, and the workflow allows the same manager to also click "approve" on their own request. What access-governance principle does this workflow violate?$q$,$q$["Segregation of duties within the provisioning process - the requestor and the approver of an access grant must be different people","Least privilege, since the manager should never be granted administrative access under any circumstances","The mover process, since this is not related to a role change","Birthright access, since administrative access should always be automatically granted"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Allowing a requestor to also approve their own request removes the independent check that provisioning approval exists to provide - a segregation-of-duties failure applied to the access-request workflow itself, letting anyone grant themselves whatever access they ask for. Whether administrative access is ever appropriate for the manager is a separate question from who approves it; this scenario involves neither a role transfer nor an automatic baseline grant.$q$),
('ci-q-1321','cissp',5,'5.5','mcq',3,$q$An organization discovers a service account with broad database access that has been active for six years, integrating two systems that were decommissioned three years ago, with no individual assigned as its owner. What governance gap allowed this account to persist unnoticed?$q$,$q$["Service accounts lack a human "mover" stage and are easily left unowned, so without an assigned owner and periodic review they can go unreviewed indefinitely","Service accounts are technically incapable of being deprovisioned once created","This is expected and acceptable behavior, since service accounts never pose a security risk","The account should have been automatically caught by MFA enforcement"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Unlike human identities, service accounts have no natural "mover" event to prompt reconsideration of their access, and if no individual is explicitly assigned ownership, no one is positioned to notice the integration it supported was retired - mature governance requires assigning an owner and periodically reviewing necessity precisely to close this gap. Service accounts can absolutely be deprovisioned, they do pose real risk as long-lived high-privilege targets, and MFA enforcement addresses authentication, not entitlement lifecycle review.$q$),
('ci-q-1322','cissp',5,'5.5','mcq',2,$q$Instead of granting a support engineer permanent administrator rights on a production database, an organization requires the engineer to request elevation, which is automatically granted for two hours and then automatically revoked. What provisioning approach does this describe, and what is its main security benefit?$q$,$q$["Just-in-time (JIT) provisioning - it minimizes the window during which an attacker who compromises the account could abuse standing elevated privilege","Birthright access - it ensures every engineer receives the same baseline access at onboarding","Static Separation of Duty - it prevents the engineer from holding two conflicting roles","Rule-Based Access Control - it applies a fixed firewall rule to database traffic"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$JIT provisioning grants an elevated privilege only for the specific window it is actually needed, then automatically revokes it, sharply shrinking the standing attack surface compared to leaving that privilege permanently active. Birthright access describes an onboarding baseline, not a temporary elevation; SSD concerns preventing conflicting role assignments; and this scenario has nothing to do with a network firewall rule.$q$),
('ci-q-1323','cissp',5,'5.5','mcq',1,$q$An identity team runs two separate types of reviews: one asks "does each specific employee still need everything currently assigned to them," and a second asks "is the 'Regional Sales Manager' role definition itself still accurate, and does everyone holding it still belong in it." What is the second type of review called?$q$,$q$["A role (entitlement) review","A user access review","An orphaned account audit","A Trojan horse assessment"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A role (entitlement) review examines a specific role or permission itself - whether its definition is still correct and whether its current population of holders still belongs - complementing rather than duplicating a user access review, which instead examines one individual's full accumulated access. An orphaned account audit targets accounts tied to departed identities, and a Trojan horse assessment is an unrelated DAC malicious-code concept.$q$),
('ci-q-1324','cissp',5,'5.5','matching',2,$q$Match each identity lifecycle stage or activity on the left to its defining action on the right.$q$,$q${"left":["Joiner","Mover","Leaver","Periodic recertification"],"right":["Grants baseline birthright access when a new identity is created","Attests whether previously granted access is still needed, on a recurring schedule","Grants access tied to a new role while revoking access tied to the prior role","Disables and eventually removes access when a person departs the organization"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,2,3,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$The joiner stage provisions the initial birthright baseline; the mover stage is defined by doing both halves of a transfer at once, granting the new role's access and revoking the old; the leaver stage disables and eventually removes access on departure; and periodic recertification is the recurring attestation check that catches anything the other three stages missed or that has become stale since the last review.$q$),
('ci-q-1325','cissp',5,'5.5','multi',2,$q$Select the TWO controls below that most directly address entitlement creep (the gradual accumulation of unrevoked access over time).$q$,$q$["Periodic access certification/recertification campaigns requiring manager or data-owner attestation","Triggering an access review and revocation of stale entitlements whenever a mover event (role or department change) occurs","Enforcing multi-factor authentication on all login attempts","Encrypting data at rest across all company file shares"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Recertification campaigns and mover-triggered reviews both directly target the mechanism behind entitlement creep - unrevoked access sitting unchecked over time - by periodically or event-drivenly forcing a fresh look at whether each entitlement is still justified. MFA strengthens authentication assurance and encryption protects data confidentiality at rest; neither one reviews or revokes accumulated standing access, so neither addresses entitlement creep itself.$q$),
('ci-q-1326','cissp',5,'5.5','ordering',2,$q$Order these identity provisioning lifecycle events as they would typically occur across one employee's time at an organization.$q$,$q$["HR's authoritative record triggers automated account creation with birthright/role-based access at onboarding (joiner)","Periodic access certification/recertification campaigns require manager attestation that existing access is still appropriate","A role change or transfer triggers granting newly required access and revoking access tied to the prior role (mover)","HR-triggered termination triggers deprovisioning, disabling and eventually removing the account (leaver)"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$The lifecycle begins with automated, HR-driven onboarding provisioning (joiner), continues with recurring recertification campaigns validating access throughout the person's tenure, is interrupted by a mover event whenever a role changes (grant new, revoke old), and concludes with leaver-triggered deprovisioning on departure - though in practice recertification continues to run repeatedly throughout, not just once, between the joiner and mover/leaver events shown here.$q$),
-- ---------- 5.6 implement and manage authentication systems ----------
('ci-q-1327','cissp',5,'5.6','mcq',1,$q$Within a Kerberos Key Distribution Center (KDC), which component issues the very first Ticket Granting Ticket (TGT) after validating a user's initial login credentials?$q$,$q$["The Authentication Service (AS)","The Ticket Granting Service (TGS)","The application server itself","The client's local operating system"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The AS handles the client's initial authentication and, once credentials are validated, issues the TGT. The TGS instead handles subsequent requests, exchanging a valid TGT for individual service tickets; the application server only ever sees a service ticket, and the client's OS is not part of the KDC.$q$),
('ci-q-1328','cissp',5,'5.6','mcq',2,$q$A Kerberos client has already obtained a Ticket Granting Ticket (TGT) earlier in its session and now needs to connect to a specific file server for the first time this session. What must the client do next, before it can present anything to the file server?$q$,$q$["Present the TGT to the Ticket Granting Service (TGS) to request a service ticket specific to that file server","Re-authenticate to the Authentication Service (AS) using the user's password again","Request a new TGT directly from the file server","Nothing further is required; the TGT alone is presented directly to the file server"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A TGT alone only proves the client already authenticated - it is not, by itself, valid for accessing a specific application server. The client must present the TGT to the TGS, which validates it and issues a service ticket scoped to that particular file server; re-entering the password is exactly what the TGT exists to avoid, and file servers never issue TGTs.$q$),
('ci-q-1329','cissp',5,'5.6','mcq',1,$q$A Kerberos-authenticated client rejects a connection to an application server because the server's response could not be validated using a key only the legitimate KDC and that specific server should know, suggesting the server may be spoofed. What Kerberos property enabled the client to detect this?$q$,$q$["Mutual authentication - the server must also prove its identity to the client, not only the reverse","Ticket time-stamping - tickets are rejected outside an allowed clock skew","The KRBTGT account's key, which only the KDC holds","Single sign-on, which lets the client avoid re-entering credentials"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Kerberos requires the server to prove its own identity back to the client using a key derived from its shared secret with the KDC, not just the client proving itself to the server - this bidirectional check is what let the client detect a server that couldn't produce a valid response. Clock-skew checking addresses replay, the KRBTGT key underlies ticket issuance generally rather than this specific server-authentication step, and SSO describes user convenience, not this detection mechanism.$q$),
('ci-q-1330','cissp',5,'5.6','mcq',3,$q$An attacker who has compromised a domain controller extracts the password hash of the KRBTGT service account and uses it to forge a Ticket Granting Ticket claiming domain administrator privileges for an arbitrary, even nonexistent, username - without ever knowing that user's actual password. What is this attack called?$q$,$q$["A golden ticket attack","A pass-the-hash attack against a single workstation","A TOCTOU race condition","A SAML assertion replay attack"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A golden ticket attack specifically involves stealing the KRBTGT account's key, the master secret the KDC uses to sign every TGT, letting the attacker forge fully valid, arbitrarily privileged tickets for any account at will - a direct consequence of the KDC being a single point of trust for the entire realm. Pass-the-hash targets a specific captured credential hash rather than forging tickets wholesale, TOCTOU is an unrelated race-condition class, and this scenario involves Kerberos tickets, not SAML assertions.$q$),
('ci-q-1331','cissp',5,'5.6','mcq',1,$q$In OAuth 2.0 terminology, which role is played by the API that actually hosts a user's protected data and accepts a presented access token to release it?$q$,$q$["The resource server","The resource owner","The client","The authorization server"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The resource server hosts the protected data and validates access tokens presented to it before granting access. The resource owner is the user who owns that data; the client is the application requesting access on the user's behalf; and the authorization server is the party that authenticates the resource owner and actually issues the access token.$q$),
('ci-q-1332','cissp',5,'5.6','mcq',2,$q$An OAuth 2.0 client's access token expires after fifteen minutes. Rather than forcing the user to log in again, the client silently presents a separate, longer-lived credential to the authorization server to obtain a fresh access token. What is that longer-lived credential called?$q$,$q$["A refresh token","A second access token","A SAML assertion","A Ticket Granting Ticket"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A refresh token is presented back to the authorization server, not the resource server, specifically to obtain a new access token without requiring the user to re-authenticate - deliberately kept separate from the short-lived access token so the high-value long-lived credential is exposed to the resource server as rarely as possible. SAML assertions and Kerberos TGTs belong to different protocols entirely.$q$),
('ci-q-1333','cissp',5,'5.6','mcq',3,$q$A mobile application implementing OAuth 2.0's Authorization Code flow cannot safely embed a client secret, since anything shipped inside the app binary can be extracted by a motivated attacker. What extension addresses this by binding the authorization code exchange to a secret generated fresh by the client itself?$q$,$q$["PKCE (Proof Key for Code Exchange)","The Client Credentials flow","SAML SP-initiated SSO","The OIDC discovery document"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$PKCE has the client generate a random code verifier and send only its hashed form when starting the flow, then requires the original verifier to complete the token exchange - protecting public clients that cannot hold a durable secret, exactly the mobile-app scenario described. The Client Credentials flow instead skips the resource owner entirely for machine-to-machine access; SAML SSO and the OIDC discovery document are unrelated mechanisms.$q$),
('ci-q-1334','cissp',5,'5.6','mcq',2,$q$A backend billing microservice needs to call an inventory microservice's API on a recurring automated schedule, with no human user involved in the transaction at all. Which OAuth 2.0 grant type is designed for exactly this machine-to-machine scenario?$q$,$q$["Client Credentials flow","Authorization Code flow","Implicit flow","SAML IdP-initiated flow"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The Client Credentials flow lets the client authenticate directly to the authorization server using its own credentials and receive a token scoped to itself, with no resource owner involved - the correct fit for service-to-service automation. The Authorization Code flow exists specifically to obtain delegated access to a human resource owner's data with their involvement; the Implicit flow (largely deprecated) also assumed a browser-based user present; and SAML IdP-initiated flow is an unrelated protocol's SSO pattern.$q$),
('ci-q-1335','cissp',5,'5.6','mcq',3,$q$A client application needs to display "Welcome, Jordan Lee" immediately after login and reliably know that the person who just authenticated is, in fact, Jordan Lee. Which OpenID Connect token is specifically designed for the client itself to consume and validate for this purpose?$q$,$q$["The ID token","The access token","A SAML assertion signed by the resource server","A refresh token"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The ID token is a JWT purpose-built for the client application to consume, asserting who authenticated and details of that authentication event - exactly what the client needs to reliably display and trust the user's identity. The access token is instead meant for the resource server to authorize API calls and does not reliably identify the user to the client; SAML assertions are signed by the Identity Provider, not a resource server; and a refresh token only obtains new access tokens.$q$),
('ci-q-1336','cissp',5,'5.6','mcq',2,$q$An employee logs into their company's central SSO portal each morning and then clicks an icon for a third-party expense-reporting application, arriving there already authenticated without any redirect back to the Identity Provider. What type of SAML SSO flow does this describe?$q$,$q$["IdP-initiated SSO - the flow starts at the Identity Provider's own portal","SP-initiated SSO - the flow starts at the Service Provider application","OAuth 2.0 Client Credentials flow","Kerberos mutual authentication"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Because the employee begins at the Identity Provider's own portal and is sent, already authenticated, directly to the Service Provider, this is IdP-initiated SSO. SP-initiated SSO instead begins at the application itself, which redirects the user to the IdP first; this scenario is SAML SSO, not an OAuth grant type or a Kerberos exchange.$q$),
('ci-q-1337','cissp',5,'5.6','multi',2,$q$Select the TWO statements below that correctly distinguish SAML's token format from the token formats commonly used by OAuth 2.0 and OpenID Connect.$q$,$q$["A SAML assertion is an XML document digitally signed by the Identity Provider","An OpenID Connect ID token, and many OAuth 2.0 access tokens, are commonly formatted as a JSON Web Token (JWT)","SAML was designed primarily for native mobile app authorization and cannot be used for browser-based SSO","OAuth 2.0 requires every access token to be an XML-signed assertion identical in format to SAML"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$SAML's defining artifact is an XML-based assertion signed by the IdP, while OIDC's ID token and many OAuth access tokens instead use the compact, JSON-based JWT format - a genuine structural difference between the protocol families. SAML's actual origin and strength is browser-based enterprise SSO, not mobile app authorization (the reverse of the claim), and OAuth 2.0 access tokens are not required to be XML-signed assertions at all; their format is left to the issuing authorization server, commonly JWT but not mandated to match SAML.$q$),
('ci-q-1338','cissp',5,'5.6','matching',2,$q$Match each authentication/authorization credential on the left to its correct description on the right.$q$,$q${"left":["Kerberos Ticket Granting Ticket (TGT)","OAuth 2.0 access token","OpenID Connect ID token","SAML assertion"],"right":["A JWT issued to a client after login proving the user's identity and authentication event to the relying party","An XML-based statement digitally signed by an Identity Provider, asserting a user's authentication or attributes to a Service Provider","A credential proving the holder already authenticated to the KDC, used to request individual service tickets without re-entering a password","A credential a client presents to a resource server to authorize access to a protected API, without necessarily identifying who the user is"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[2,3,0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$A TGT proves prior authentication to the KDC and is exchanged for service tickets, never for API access directly; an OAuth access token authorizes API calls to a resource server but is not designed to reliably identify the user; an OIDC ID token is the JWT purpose-built to prove identity and the authentication event to the client itself; and a SAML assertion is the XML-based, IdP-signed statement of authentication or attributes presented to a Service Provider. Each credential belongs to a different protocol family solving a related but distinct problem.$q$),
('ci-q-1339','cissp',5,'5.6','ordering',2,$q$Order the steps of a Kerberos client authenticating and then accessing an application server, from first to last.$q$,$q$["The client sends an authentication request to the Authentication Service (AS) using the user's credentials","The AS verifies the credentials and returns a Ticket Granting Ticket (TGT) encrypted for the client","The client presents the TGT to the Ticket Granting Service (TGS) to request a service ticket for a specific application server","The TGS validates the TGT and issues a service ticket for that specific server","The client presents the service ticket directly to the application server to establish an authenticated session"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3,4]$q$::jsonb,$q$Kerberos authentication always begins with the AS exchange, trading initial credentials for a TGT so the password itself is used only once; every subsequent server access reuses that TGT at the TGS to obtain a fresh, server-specific service ticket, which is what the client ultimately presents to the application server itself - the TGT is never presented directly to an application server.$q$);
